<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cephissus (Boeotia)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kifisos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cephissus is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that rises near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-parnassus">Mount Parnassus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and flows east through Phocis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. In antiquity it fed the marshy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a>, which was drained in the late 19th century. Its valley was the heart of ancient Phocis, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elateia">Elateia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cephissus-boeotia.jpg" alt="Cephissus (Boeotia)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Costas78 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voiotikos_Kiphisos01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Boeotian Cephissus, so called to distinguish it from other rivers of the same name, rises at Lilaia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> on the north-western slope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-parnassus">Mount Parnassus</a>. It flows east through the Boeotian plain past the towns of Amfikleia, Kato Tithorea and Orchomenos, and its drainage basin covers 1,958 square kilometres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a>, into which it emptied, was therefore also called the Cephisian Lake.</p><p>A post-glacial river, the Cephissus never carried enough water to cut a clear channel to the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. Its main flow was seasonal meltwater, which gathered in a system of three lakes in the Boeotian lowlands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a> was never more than a wetland a few feet deep. From it, water passed through katabothra, caves in the karst limestone, to Lake Yliki, the ancient Hylice, and on underground to Lake Paralimni, which hangs above the gulf; there were no surface channels between the lakes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Greek mythology the river god Cephissus was associated with the river. Pausanias records a Theban tradition that the river once ran under a mountain to the sea until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> blocked the passage and turned the water into the Orchomenian plain. He also says that on certain days the people of Lilaia threw cakes and other customary offerings into the river's spring, and that these reappeared in the Castalian Spring.</p><p>The valley links northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, through the passes of Mount Oeta and Mount Kallidromo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> among them, with southern Greece and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. For that reason the rulers of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> built a chain of forts and watchtowers along its course in the Frankish period.</p><p>Draining the lakes was a large engineering task of the 19th and early 20th centuries. Copais was turned into farmland in 1887, revealing the katabothra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> works built to stem the flow, and an artificial outflow now leads to Lake Yliki. The opened underground paths raised Yliki enough to drown all traces of Bronze Age settlement in its valley, and in the later 20th century the waters of the two remaining lakes were taken for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> aqueduct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cephissus (Boeotia)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Phocians lived in the valley of the <b>Cephissus</b> and around Mount Parnassus, in some twenty small towns such as Elateia.</blockquote><a href="/state/phocian-league">Phocian League</a></li></ul></section>
